  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $f:X\\to Y$ and $g:Y\\to Z$. Prove the following:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $g\\circ f$ is surjective, then $g$ is surjective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Every $z\\in Z$ has the form $g(f(x))$ for some $x\\in X$. Thus $z=g(y)$ for $y=f(x)\\in Y$, so $g$ is surjective.",
          "worked": "Let $z\\in Z$. Since $g\\circ f$ is surjective, there exists $x\\in X$ such that $g(f(x))=z$. Set $y=f(x)\\in Y$. Then $g(y)=z$. Every element of $Z$ therefore has a preimage in $Y$, so $g$ is surjective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $g\\circ f$ is surjective, then $f$ need not be surjective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Take $X=Z=\\{1\\}$, $Y=\\{1,2\\}$, $f(1)=1$ and $g(1)=g(2)=1$.\n\nThen $g\\circ f$ is surjective, but $2$ has no preimage under $f$.",
          "worked": "Let $X=\\{1\\}$, $Y=\\{1,2\\}$ and $Z=\\{1\\}$. Define $f:X\\to Y$ by $f(1)=1$, and $g:Y\\to Z$ by $g(1)=g(2)=1$.\n\nSince $g(f(1))=g(1)=1$, the range of $g\\circ f$ is $\\{1\\}=Z$, so $g\\circ f$ is surjective. However, the range of $f$ is $\\{1\\}$, which omits $2\\in Y$. Thus $f$ is not surjective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
